{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Until Shelah my son grows up.</b> Because there was no older brother than him remaining, he said to her, 'until Shelah my son grows up'; but had there been an older brother there, he would not have said 'until Shelah grows up.' From here our Rabbis of blessed memory said: if one ties the matter to a minor — until he grows up — or to an adult — until he returns from overseas — or to a deaf-mute or a fool — until he regains his senses — we do not listen to him. And as for what Scripture says (Ruth 1:11): ״שובנה בנותי למה תלכנה עמי העוד לי בנים במעי והיו לכם לאנשים״ — and there are those who maintain that from this it follows that had she had sons in her womb born after the death of their brothers, they would be subject to levirate marriage — this is not so. For Scripture explicitly says (Deuteronomy 25:5): ״כי ישבו אחים יחדו״ — when brothers dwell together — meaning they had one shared existence in the world, excluding the wife of a brother who was not in the world." "<b>For he said, lest he too die like his brothers.</b> For she is established in the presumption that her husbands die, as they said: after two times it is a presumption — thus Rashi of blessed memory explained. And the Ramban wrote on this: if so, why would Judah deceive her, when she is forbidden to Shelah? And as they said regarding marriage: after two times it is a presumption. Therefore he explained that Shelah would be fit for levirate marriage, but his father did not want him to perform it, and he was still a youth, for Judah was concerned lest he destroy his seed and be liable to death, since he was a minor and not fit to beget; and when she saw that Shelah had grown up in her eyes, while he was still a youth in his father's eyes, then she did this deed."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:18:39.340771191Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}